                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "I got to play this masterpiece during one of the lowest points of my life, I was depressed. At first, I thought it would just be a game to distract me from everything I was going through: all the changes, the difficult decisions, and the overwhelming uncertainty of it all. I wasn’t really in a place where I could consciously learn anything from an experience like this. I just wanted an escape.\n\nBut somehow, Outer Wilds became so much more than that. It became my main coping mechanism.\n\nI had intense astrophobia and megalophobia, so exploring this universe wasn’t exactly a comfortable experience for me. And yet, I was intrigued. I was curious. I was exhausted, but completely invested in the journey. Captivated by the beautiful soundtrack, the storytelling, the puzzles, and the sheer wonder of it all, I kept going.\n\nAnd I’m so, so glad I did.\n\nBecause somewhere along the way, without even realizing it, I started learning. About myself, about life, about change, about letting go, and about accepting the things I cannot control. This game found me at a time when I needed it most, and in its own quiet, extraordinary way, it helped me find my way out of depression.\n\nI will forever be grateful to Mobius Digital for creating what has become my favorite game of all time, an experience I can only truly have once. I think about it every single day, and it still brings tears to my eyes.\n\nNot tears of sadness, though. The kind of tears you shed when something touches a part of you that you didn’t even know needed healing. The kind you cry when you realize that something, somehow, has changed your life forever.\n\nThank you, Outer Wilds. For everything.",
